# Status line recovery
This reference is the single description of why a dashboard status line
disappears, how to diagnose it, and how to recover it. `SKILL.md`,
`/jstack:dashboard-repair`, and the header of `scripts/install-statusline.mjs`
summarize it and point here.
Recovery is off by default. A user runs `install` or `repair` explicitly, or
opts in once per Claude config directory for automatic recovery. Nothing here
names a particular external tool, machine, or operating system: causes are
reported as types with evidence.
## Diagnose first
Run the read-only report. It never writes a file.
```bash
node scripts/install-statusline.mjs status [--config-dir DIR]...
```
The script ships in the installed package's `payload` directory beside this
reference; inside a Claude command it is
`${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/dashboard/scripts/install-statusline.mjs`.
Without `--config-dir` it reads `${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:-$HOME/.claude}`. Pass
`--config-dir` once per Claude home to check several; the output then has a
`homes` array. A setting in one home says nothing about another.
Read these fields:
| Field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `healthy` | `true` when the command is this package's shim, the shim is current, the build resolves, and no selected snapshot is behind. Stop here when it is `true`. |
| `owner` | `ours`, `empty`, `known-managed-by-another-tool`, or `user` |
| `identity` | For another tool's value, the plugin identity derived from its path, or the name the user declared |
| `causes` | Cause types with evidence; see the table below |
| `resolves`, `installation`, `version` | The build a render loads and the installed package it came from; `null` means no build in this config directory |
| `resolvedFrom` | `metadata` when the host installation registry named the package; `cache-scan` when the registry was unreadable and a bounded cache scan found it |
| `optin` | The automatic recovery record: `autoRepair`, `autoRepairEffective`, `allowReplace`, `syncGlobs`, `codex` |
| `preferred` | `false` when the running copy is not the installation the resolver prefers; that copy never repairs automatically |
| `codex` | The Codex `tui.status_line` report for `$CODEX_HOME` |
## Ownership classes
| Class | What it covers | Repair |
|---|---|---|
| `ours` | This package's shim, or a build path inside this package | Repaired when broken |
| `empty` | No `statusLine` command | Filled in |
| `known-managed-by-another-tool` | A command inside another plugin's cache or data directory, or one the user declared with `--known-managed ID=SUBSTRING` | Preserved until the user allows that identity |
| `user` | Anything else | Never replaced by repair |
## Cause types
| Cause | Symptom | What it means | Recovery |
|---|---|---|---|
| `whole-file-restoration` | The status line vanished after it had worked | `statusLine` is absent but the opt-in record shows this package installed it; the whole settings file was restored from an older copy | `repair`; if the restore source is a sibling file, keep it in sync with `syncGlobs` |
| `ownership-contention` | A different status line appears | After this package installed its command, a user or another tool's value took the slot | Nothing is written. Allow a tool identity with `optin --allow-replace ID`; a user value is replaced only through setup with the user's agreement |
| `identity-mismatch` | The line renders nothing or an old build | The command is this package's shim but belongs to another installation's data directory, such as a copy installed under a different marketplace name | `repair` points it at the preferred installation's shim |
| `pinned-version` | The line stays on an old version after an update | The command points directly at one version's build | `repair` replaces it with the shim, which follows updates |
| `installation-removed` | The line disappears after an update or cache cleanup | The command is ours, but the build it names is gone, or no build is installed | Update or reinstall jstack in this config directory, then `repair` |
| `shim-missing` | The line is blank | The shim file the command runs does not exist | `repair` writes it |
| `shim-stale` | Behavior lags behind the installed version | The shim differs from this package's shim | `repair` rewrites it |
| `status-line-type` | The command is right but nothing runs | `statusLine.type` is not `command` | `repair` fixes the type |
| `not-configured` | No status line was ever set up | `statusLine` is absent and no installation was recorded | Run `/jstack:dashboard-setup`, or `install` |
| `no-build` | `repair` exits 1 | No dashboard build resolves under this config directory's plugins | Install or update jstack in this config directory; do not add a link or a pinned path |
| `settings-unparsable` | `repair` exits 2 | `settings.json` cannot be read as JSON | Nothing is written. Fix the file, then `repair` |
| `snapshot-out-of-sync` | Restoring a snapshot brings back an old status line | A sibling file selected by `syncGlobs` lacks this package's command | `repair` updates the selected snapshots |
| `codex-status-line-lost` | The Codex footer reverted to its default | `tui.status_line` is absent but a layout was recorded | `repair --codex` or `codex repair` restores the recorded layout |
## Repair
```bash
node scripts/install-statusline.mjs repair [--config-dir DIR]... [--codex]
```
Repair reads `settings.json` again right before writing, saves a backup beside it,
writes through a temporary file and a rename, and keeps the file's permissions.
A healthy directory is left untouched and the result is `action: unchanged`.
| Exit | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `0` | Healthy, repaired, or reported |
| `1` | No build the shim can resolve |
| `2` | A configuration file could not be parsed, or changed during repair; nothing was written |
| `3` | The value belongs to the user or to another tool that is not allowed; nothing was written |
| `4` | A Codex `config.toml` layout this script does not edit safely; nothing was written |
A repair started by the session-start hook or by `watch` uses trigger `hook`: it
runs only when automatic recovery is effective, prints nothing, and always exits 0 so
that a session can start. Its result is visible through the next `status`.
## Opt-in settings
The record lives beside the installed shim in
`<config dir>/plugins/data/<data id>/statusline-repair.json` and is written only
through `optin`:
```bash
node scripts/install-statusline.mjs optin [--config-dir DIR] --auto-repair on
node scripts/install-statusline.mjs optin --allow-replace IDENTITY
node scripts/install-statusline.mjs optin --known-managed ID=SUBSTRING
node scripts/install-statusline.mjs optin --sync-glob "settings.json.PATTERN"
node scripts/install-statusline.mjs optin --codex on
```
- `--auto-repair on` enables recovery at session start for this config directory.
`JSTACK_DASHBOARD_STATUSLINE=1` enables it for one environment regardless of the
record, and `JSTACK_DASHBOARD_STATUSLINE=0` disables it regardless of the record.
- `--allow-replace IDENTITY` lets repair replace that tool's value; use the
`identity` from `status`. `--deny-replace` removes it. Allowing
`known-managed-by-another-tool` covers every tool and is rarely what a user wants.
- `--sync-glob` selects sibling files of `settings.json` that another process uses
as restore sources. Repair sets the same command in a selected file only when its
own status line is empty or ours. It is off until a pattern is given;
`--clear-sync-globs` removes the patterns.
- `--codex on` records the current Codex layout so a lost `tui.status_line` can be
restored. Codex repair adds the key only when it is missing: into the existing
`[tui]` table, as a dotted `tui.status_line` at the root, or as one new `[tui]`
table. It never replaces an existing value, including one chosen through the Codex
status line picker, and it refuses an inline `tui` table, a duplicate `[tui]`, or
`[[tui]]` with exit 4. Codex configuration is never created where no Codex home
exists.
- Several Claude homes each need their own opt-in: repeat the command with each
`--config-dir`.
## Keeping a watcher running
```bash
node scripts/install-statusline.mjs watch [--config-dir DIR]... [--interval SECONDS]
```
`watch` stays in the foreground. A change to `settings.json` or a sibling file is
followed by a short settle delay, then a repair with trigger `hook`. A periodic pass
(default 300 seconds, minimum 10) catches a replacement by rename that a file
watcher can miss. It acts only for directories whose automatic recovery is
effective and prints one line only when it repairs.
jstack installs no operating-system service. To keep it running between sessions,
start this command from whatever the user already uses to run programs at login
or keep them alive, restart it when it exits, and pass every config directory it
should cover. Running it inside a terminal multiplexer session is the simplest form.
## Moving an existing machine to this version
1. Update jstack in every profile through the route used to install it (see the
repository README), then open a new session in each.
2. For each Claude home, run `status --config-dir DIR`. Confirm that `resolves`
lies inside the new version and that `resolvedFrom` is `metadata`.
3. Run `repair --config-dir DIR`. Pinned commands and shims from another
installation are repointed; user and other tools' values are reported, not
changed.
4. Opt in where recovery is wanted: `optin --config-dir DIR --auto-repair on`.
Add `--allow-replace` only for a tool identity the user chooses to override, and
`--sync-glob` only for restore sources the user names.
5. For Codex, run `optin --codex on` after choosing the layout, then
`repair --codex` to confirm.
6. Optionally keep `watch` running with every config directory.
7. Remove manual workarounds. List links in the plugin cache with
`find DIR/plugins/cache -maxdepth 2 -type l`, and look for status line commands
edited by hand to name a version directory. Remove one workaround at a time and
run `status` again. When it stays `healthy: true` with `resolvedFrom: metadata`,
the workaround is no longer needed. When it reports `no-build`, reinstall jstack in
that directory instead of restoring the link.
